    "narrative": "Today\u2019s optimal build for Ghost Ride the WHIP leans heavily on high-ceiling matchups against middling starters, with **James Wood** (70.96) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (70.58) leading the charge. **James Wood** is our clear top play; batting leadoff against Bradley provides an ideal runway for production, and his stellar .377 xwOBA paired with a favorable park factor makes him the focal point of the lineup. Similarly, **Vinnie Pasquantino** is perfectly positioned in the #3 spot against Williams, benefiting from a massive +14% platoon adjustment that pushes his efficiency score comfortably into the elite tier. **Corbin Carroll** (70.10) remains a mandatory start; despite facing a respectable arm in Chandler, his elite speed profile and top-of-the-order utility against a righty remain indispensable to our SB-heavy philosophy.\n\nWe are navigating several complex platoon and velocity-based decisions today. **Seiya Suzuki** (68.46) stands out as an intriguing play; while he faces Abbott, his score is bolstered by a +10% SP skill modifier and, crucially, a slow pitcher delivery that bumps his SB potential\u2014a vital consideration given our focus on the basepaths. Conversely, **Austin Riley** (47.22) and **Dalton Rushing** (49.35) are on the lower end of our active spectrum. **Austin Riley** struggles against the high-skill profile of Kirby, while **Dalton Rushing** sits near the floor due to the neutral-to-tough matchup against Lambert. We are monitoring **Tyler Soderstrom** (48.55) closely, as his moderate score is entirely dependent on favorable wind conditions offsetting a significant -19% penalty against the pitcher's skill. \n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Alex Freeland** (40.41), who hovered just above the 40-point threshold but ultimately lacked the counting-stat leverage of our starting infielders. We opted to stay disciplined, benching **Jose Caballero** (39.32) and **Luis Campusano** (37.26) as they failed to clear our \"Zebras Floor,\" primarily due to poor platoon splits and the high-skill profiles of their respective matchups (deGrom and Webb). The primary risk for the squad today is **Mickey Moniak**, who is effectively ruled out due to a rainout, and the lingering volatility surrounding **Austin Riley**\u2019s matchup against a top-tier arm in Kirby. We remain confident that the aggressive starts for **Trevor Story** (59.08) and **Josh Naylor** (63.81) provide the necessary stability to navigate this slate without exposing the roster to further below-floor risks.",
